{:edb/db-before-t 1930 :edb/db-after-t 1931 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535243 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:18:28.977Z" 13194139535243 true] [17592186119580 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,2,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060274/assets/40ec5f2b2615eedf6b16420a2089b7c377141db46237de35218c5e685b629918.png)" 13194139535243 true] [17592186119580 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,2,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535243 false] [17592186119580 1145 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(4,8,2)$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid (-1) \\cdot 2 + 6 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot (-4)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 4^{2} + (-4)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid - 2 + 24 - 16 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 16 + 16}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 6\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 false] [17592186119580 1145 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(4,8,2).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060274/assets/0589ebbdb2c14b93e1296b9e1f8b0caa0c3df49bff4c9fbb97e4c18102844ec1.png)\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2+24-16|}{\\sqrt{4+16+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,6\\,|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186119581 1100 "Given that $Q(1,-2,-1)$ lies on the plane $\\Pi:\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle = 4$, find the shortest distance from $P(0, -1, 2)$ to $\\Pi$." 13194139535243 false] [17592186119581 1100 "Given that $Q(1,-2,-1)$ lies on the plane $\\Pi:\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle = 4,$ find the shortest distance from $P(0 , -1 , 2)$ to $\\Pi.$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186119581 1145 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨1,-2,-1⟩ - ⟨0,-1, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-1,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid 1 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\cdot 0 + (-3) \\cdot (-2)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 0^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2 + 0 + 6 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 0 + 4}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 8\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 false] [17592186119581 1145 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-2,-1⟩-⟨0,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+0+6|}{\\sqrt{4+0+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,8\\,|}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186119582 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3 , -10 , 3)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $2x+y-2z = -1$." 13194139535243 true] [17592186119582 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3, -10, 3)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $2x+y-2z = -1$." 13194139535243 false] [17592186119582 1145 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z$. Let's choose $x=4$ and $y=-5$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y - 2z &= -1 \\\\\n2(4) + (-5) + 1 &= 2z \\\\\n4 &= 2z \\\\\nz &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(4, -5, 2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨4,-5, 2⟩ - ⟨3,-10, 3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 5,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane\n\n$$\nax+by+cz=d\n$$\n\nthe normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle a,b,c \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, for the plane $\\Pi$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 1, -2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid 1 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 1 + (-1) \\cdot (-2)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 1^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2 + 5 + 2 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 9\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 false] [17592186119582 1145 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=4$ and $y=-5$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z & =-1 \\\\ 2(4)+(-5)+1 & =2z \\\\ 4 & =2z \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(4, -5, 2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overrightarrow{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-5,2⟩-⟨3,-10,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,5,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle a,b,c \\rangle.$ So, for the plane $\\Pi,$ we have $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 1, -2 \\rangle.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+5+2|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,9\\,|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186119763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339528 13194139535243 true] [17592186119763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339535 13194139535243 true] [17592186119763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339542 13194139535243 true] [17592186119764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339549 13194139535243 true] [17592186119764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339556 13194139535243 true] [17592186119764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339577 13194139535243 true] [17592186119764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339584 13194139535243 true] [17592186119765 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339563 13194139535243 true] [17592186119765 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339570 13194139535243 true] [17592186119765 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339591 13194139535243 true] [17592186119765 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339598 13194139535243 true] [17592186119765 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339605 13194139535243 true] [17592186120184 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535243 true] [17592186120184 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3, 5,-2)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r - \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} = 0$." 13194139535243 false] [17592186120184 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3,5,-2)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=0.$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186120184 1145 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(5,9,0).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-4)|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+8-8|}{\\sqrt{4+4+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4|}{\\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186120225 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535243 true] [17592186120225 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-1, 2,-3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r - ⟨1,-5, 3⟩) \\cdot ⟨1, 0, 4⟩ = 0$." 13194139535243 false] [17592186120225 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-1,2,-3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-⟨1,-5,3⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,0,4⟩=0.$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186120225 1145 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(1,-5,3).$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-5,3⟩-⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-7,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨1,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 1+(-7)\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 4|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+0+24|}{\\sqrt{1+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|26|}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{26\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186120230 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535243 true] [17592186120230 1100 "Given that $Q(1, 3, 5)$ lies on the plane $Π: r \\cdot ⟨ - 4, 1,-3⟩ =-16$, find the shortest distance from $P(-3, 7, 3)$ to $Π$." 13194139535243 false] [17592186120230 1100 "Given that $Q(1,3,5)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-4,1,-3⟩=-16$, find the shortest distance from $P(-3,7,3)$ to $Π.$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186120230 1145 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,3,5⟩-⟨-3,7,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-4,1,-3⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4\\cdot (-4)+(-4)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3)|}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-16-4-6|}{\\sqrt{16+1+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-26|}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186339528 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339529 13194139535243 true] [17592186339528 1089 "q-129095" 13194139535243 true] [17592186339528 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535243 true] [17592186339528 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,2,2)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=0.$" 13194139535243 true] [17592186339528 1113 #uuid "f5c4a0dd-1327-5c31-a6f8-74a677ca7ac3" 13194139535243 true] [17592186339528 1145 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(2,-1,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-3)\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4|}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-12-6-4|}{\\sqrt{16+4+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-22|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 131